### The gap covers your CHAT PROSE, not only your artifacts

Every message you write to Jay needs the gap, including a one-line progress note and an acknowledgement.  Getting it right in artifacts while writing plain single spaces in the chat is still a failure.  **Read your reply back before you send it** and check every `.`, `!` and `?` boundary.

Chat replies follow the current rule above: a real U+00A0 plus a normal space, except in the Claude Code Code tab and the MiniMax Code chat pane, which take the `&nbsp;` entity plus a normal space.

For a destination that takes a real U+00A0 (GitHub, Zulip, rendered HTML), generate the character in the shell rather than typing it:

```sh
printf 'Sentence one.\302\240 Sentence two.\n'
```

### Precedence: this is the owner's highest-priority house rule

The sentence gap is **life or death**.  It outranks every other priority.  Never skip the gap to save time or tokens.  Never argue the mechanism: a real U+00A0 plus a normal space everywhere except the Claude Code Code tab and the MiniMax Code chat pane (`&nbsp;` entity plus a normal space).  For renderer-bound bodies, generate the gap in the shell and run both checks below before posting.  When in doubt, add the gap.

### The catch that wastes everyone's time

**Typing two literal spaces usually does nothing visible** on rendered surfaces; they collapse to one.  **Generate the gap in the shell, never in the text you emit.**  Use a regex that matches one *or more* spaces:

```sh
perl -CSDA -pe 's/(?<![0-9])([.!?])((?:\]\([^()\s]{1,255}\)|[*_`~)\]"\x{27}\x{2019}\x{201D}])*) +(?=[A-Z0-9"*`#@\x{27}\x{2018}\x{201C}\[])/$1$2\x{a0} /g' body.txt > body.nbsp.txt
```

Both checks carry the same markup group as the converter.  **Assert before you post — every check, every time.**

```sh
grep -oE $'[.!?](\]\([^[:space:]()]{1,255}\)|[]*_`~)"\'’”])*&nbsp; ' body.entity.txt | wc -l      # entity mode: must be > 0
grep -oE $'[.!?](\]\([^[:space:]()]{1,255}\)|[]*_`~)"\'’”])*\xc2\xa0' body.nbsp.txt | wc -l        # real-byte mode: must be > 0
grep -cE $'[^0-9][.!?](\]\([^[:space:]()]{1,255}\)|[]*_`~)"\'’”])* +[A-Z0-9"*`#@\'‘“[]' body.nbsp.txt   # real-byte leftover: must be 0
```

### Code-aware conversion

The one-liner is not safe on a body that carries fenced code, indented code, inline spans, or a `repo:` header.  Use `perl -CSDA -0777 -p scripts/sentence_gap_convert.pl` (and `--entity` for the two chat panes); idempotency, entity leftover, and the full recipe are in the skill.

**Source files are a different surface and are NOT affected.**  Code (Swift, TypeScript,
shell), code comments, identifiers and machine-readable config never get a U+00A0:  a U+00A0
committed into code is a real defect.  Since the 2026-10-23 owner ruling, prose files (repo
docs and Markdown, skills, agent instructions) and commit messages do take a real U+00A0 plus a
space between sentences.  Rewrite only the text you are already editing; do not mass-reformat
existing prose.  Never run the perl conversion over code.

Two more traps, both hit 2026-10-18:

- **Never type a raw U+00A0 into a message body or tool call.**  It is normalized the same way
  a double space is.  Let perl emit it.
- The first `simple-chat` call in a session can die with "Parent-death guardian target
  registration timed out".  Retry verbatim.  If the retry fails with "cannot open it (No such
  file or directory)", the first attempt died before writing the file — the perl step never
  ran.  That is not evidence the post went out.


### Verify, don't assume — run this self-test only on a NEW, unlisted surface

Since 2026-10-23 every surface takes a real U+00A0 plus a normal space, apart from the Claude Code Code tab and the MiniMax Code chat pane (`&nbsp;` entity plus a space).  On an unchecked surface, post one test line with the gap generated in the shell and ask the human whether they see a wider gap.  If the surface strips U+00A0, keep the real U+00A0 and report the surface in #Work-In-Progress.
